\begin{theorem}
\label{courseIII:almost-complex-structures-and-integrability:source-theorem-9.1}
A smooth almost complex structure is induced by a complex coordinate atlas if and only if its tensor \eqref{courseIII:almost-complex-structures-and-integrability:eq:1.5} vanishes.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Necessity was proved in \cref{courseIII:almost-complex-structures-and-integrability:section-1}. For sufficiency, the original zero tensor gives \eqref{courseIII:almost-complex-structures-and-integrability:eq:6.5}; \cref{courseIII:almost-complex-structures-and-integrability:section-7,courseIII:almost-complex-structures-and-integrability:section-8} construct the actual divergence-free analytic coefficient field, and the commuting flows give \eqref{courseIII:almost-complex-structures-and-integrability:eq:9.5}. On overlaps the chain rule and the Cauchy integral argument after \eqref{courseIII:almost-complex-structures-and-integrability:eq:1.3} make the transitions holomorphic. No analytic regularity hypothesis was imposed on the original smooth field.
\end{proof}
